<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anita Garibaldi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ana Maria de Jesus Ribeiro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anita Garibaldi, born Ana Maria de Jesus Ribeiro in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, was the companion and later wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>. She met him at Laguna in 1839 and fought beside him in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragamuffin-war">Ragamuffin War</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> and in the defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in 1849. She died during the retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anita-garibaldi.jpg" alt="Anita Garibaldi" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gaetano Gallino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anita_Garibaldi_-_1839.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ana Maria de Jesus Ribeiro was born on 30 August 1821 in Laguna, then part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, a year before the country's independence. Her family was poor and of Azorean Portuguese descent. She was the third of ten children of Maria Antônia de Jesus Antunes and Bento Ribeiro da Silva, a tropeiro. In 1835, at fourteen, she was forced to marry Manuel Duarte Aguiar, who abandoned her to join the Imperial Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>, a Niçois sailor of Ligurian descent, had fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1836 and was fighting for the separatist Riograndense Republic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragamuffin-war">Ragamuffin War</a>. Anita joined him on his ship, the Rio Pardo, in October 1839, and a month later first saw action in battles at Imbituba and Laguna. A skilled horsewoman, she is said to have taught him about the gaúcho culture of the Pampas.</p><p>At the Battle of Curitibanos Garibaldi became separated from the front and Anita was captured. Her guards told her that he was dead, and she was allowed to search among the dead but did not find him. She then escaped on a horse, was pursued, and had her horse shot, and she waded the river Canoas. She spent four days in the woods without food or drink before reaching the rebels and was reunited with Garibaldi at Vacaria. Their first child, Menotti, was born a few months later. They married on 26 March 1842 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Anita went with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> and his red-shirted legionnaires to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a>. In February 1849 he joined the defence of the newly proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> against Neapolitan and French intervention, and she joined him there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> fell to a French siege on 30 June, and she fled with the Garibaldian Legion. Pregnant and sick with malaria, she died on 4 August 1849 in her husband's arms at Guiccioli Farm in Mandriole, near Ravenna. Her body had to be hurriedly buried.</p><p>In 1860 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> rode out to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teanum-sidicinum">Teano</a> to hail Victor Emanuel II as king of a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> wearing Anita's striped scarf. She is a symbol of Brazilian republicanism and was recognised as a national heroine after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>. Laguna has a museum dedicated to her. In 1932 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> had a monument to her raised on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janiculum">Janiculum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, near the equestrian statue of her husband.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anita Garibaldi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Laguna, Garibaldi met <b>Ana Maria de Jesus Ribeiro</b>, known as Anita, who joined him in battle.</blockquote><a href="/state/juliana-republic">Juliana Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-santa-catarina">Laguna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. Died in Mandriole, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-28.48,-48.78&amp;year=1839" aria-label="Laguna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Anita Garibaldi"><image href="/maps/globes/g300s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5838 4911h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5838 4911h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-28.48,-48.78&amp;year=1839">What was here in 1839? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
